 Climate change reaches everyone, but it does not reach everyone equally. Heat waves, wildfire smoke, and flooding are harder on older bodies, and existing health conditions make the risk sharper still.
 
 That is one reason seniors have a stake in this. The other is that we have time, experience, credibility with decision-makers, and a long view of what we are handing on. The resources here cover both sides: how to protect yourself and the people around you, and why our generation's voice matters in this fight.
-
-## Resources
-
-- [10 reasons why Seniors must step up for Climate Action](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1I0n6H4iO4Dfm5xVGy_r7-fejokdYOpPa/view?usp=drive_link) — Seniors for Climate BC (PDF) — Our own short guide to why you should get involved.
-- [Healthy Aging in a Changing Climate](https://climatechange.pathways.med.ubc.ca/advice-from-the-experts/healthy-aging-in-a-changing-climate/) — UBC Faculty of Medicine — Expert advice on aging well as the climate shifts.
-- [How older adults can reduce risks of climate change hazards](https://bchealthycommunities.ca/index.php/2024/04/04/how-older-adults-can-reduce-risks-of-climate-change-hazards/) — BC Healthy Communities — Why older adults are more vulnerable during extreme weather, plus BC resources for planning and preparedness.
-- [Why climate change is a health crisis for older adults in Canada](https://entente.ca/why-climate-change-is-a-health-crisis-for-older-adults-in-canada/) — Entente Education Canada — The Canadian picture, framed as a health issue.
-- [Older adults are uniquely impacted by the climate crisis](https://www.climaterealityproject.org/blog/older-adults-are-uniquely-impacted-climate-crisis-heres-why) — The Climate Reality Project — A plain explanation of the particular risks older people face.
